## Answer

**The maximum permitted assembly U-value for a residential exterior mass wall in Climate Zone 3 according to IECC-2012 is 0.104 Btu/h-ft²-F.**

This requirement is sourced from IECC-2012 Table C402.1.2 and applies to "Typical Insulated Exterior Mass Wall" construction.

### Context
For comparison, here are the other residential exterior wall U-value requirements in Climate Zone 3 under IECC-2012:
- **Exterior Mass Wall**: 0.104 Btu/h-ft²-F
- **Steel Framed Exterior Wall**: 0.064 Btu/h-ft²-F
- **Wood Framed Exterior Wall**: 0.064 Btu/h-ft²-F
- **Metal Building Wall**: 0.052 Btu/h-ft²-F

Mass walls have a higher (less stringent) U-value requirement than framed walls, which is typical in building codes as mass walls have different thermal performance characteristics than framed construction.

**Note:** The database contains Climate Zone 3 as a single zone. IECC-2012 actually subdivides Climate Zone 3 into 3A (warm-humid) and 3B (warm-dry), which may have different requirements in the actual standard. You may want to consult the official IECC-2012 Table C402.1.2 directly to confirm the specific requirement for 3A versus 3B.
